2186432135 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186432117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432135 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fcae4ef9-b956-5a61-8936-0e4dd16f2526" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432136 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432136 #edb/ref 1069 "q-57343" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432136 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432136 #edb/ref 1071 "$6÷\\frac{3}{5}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432136 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "13ffbc44-e82a-541b-9a69-eaa776d03a61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432136 #edb/ref 1074 "Dividing a whole number by a fraction is equivalent to multiplying the whole number by the reciprocal of the fraction.\n\nFirst, we write $6$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n6=\\frac{6}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we note that the reciprocal of $\\frac{3}{5}$ is $\\frac{5}{3}.$ \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n6÷\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{6}{1}÷\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{6}{1}\\times \\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply our fractions. \n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{1}\\times \\frac{5}{3}=\\frac{6\\times 5}{1\\times 3}=\\frac{30}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n30÷3=10\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432137 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432137 #edb/ref 1069 "q-57353" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432137 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432137 #edb/ref 1071 "$7÷\\frac{3}{4}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432137 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "422627b7-b37a-52a4-aa47-e207326c7814" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432137 #edb/ref 1074 "Dividing a whole number by a fraction is equivalent to multiplying the whole number by the reciprocal of the fraction.\n\nFirst, we write $7$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n7=\\frac{7}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we note that the reciprocal of $\\frac{3}{4}$ is $\\frac{4}{3}.$ \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n7÷\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{7}{1}÷\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{7}{1}\\times \\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply our fractions. \n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{1}\\times \\frac{4}{3}=\\frac{7\\times 4}{1\\times 3}=\\frac{28}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{28}{3}=9\\,\\text{R}1=9\\,\\frac{1}{3}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432138 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432138 #edb/ref 1069 "q-57187" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432138 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432138 #edb/ref 1071 "$4÷\\frac{5}{7}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432138 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "44133494-469d-5dbd-8472-1b49df14681b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432138 #edb/ref 1074 "Dividing a whole number by a fraction is equivalent to multiplying the whole number by the reciprocal of the fraction.\n\nFirst, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4=\\frac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we note that the reciprocal of $\\frac{5}{7}$ is $\\frac{7}{5}.$ \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n4÷\\frac{5}{7}=\\frac{4}{1}÷\\frac{5}{7}=\\frac{4}{1}\\times \\frac{7}{5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply our fractions. \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{1}\\times \\frac{7}{5}=\\frac{4\\times 7}{1\\times 5}=\\frac{28}{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{28}{5}=5\\,\\text{R}3=5\\,\\frac{3}{5}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432139 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432139 #edb/ref 1069 "q-211475" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432139 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432139 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed as an mixed number in its lowest terms, $3÷\\frac{6}{7}=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432139 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74bacbf6-b03a-5e84-a6aa-342811a3c626" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432139 #edb/ref 1074 "Dividing a whole number by a fraction is equivalent to multiplying the whole number by the reciprocal of the fraction.\n\nFirst, we write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we note that the reciprocal of $\\frac{6}{7}$ is $\\frac{7}{6}.$ \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n3÷\\frac{6}{7}=\\frac{3}{1}÷\\frac{6}{7}=\\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{7}{6}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply our fractions. Note that we can simplify the process by swapping the denominators first.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{7}{6} & =\\frac{3}{6}\\times \\frac{7}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\times 7 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert our result to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{2}=3\\,\\text{R}\\,1=3\\,\\frac{1}{2}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432140 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432140 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99937" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432140 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432140 #edb/ref 1071 "$8÷\\frac{3}{7}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432140 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7c0abb3e-b307-5eda-92d0-4b63963ecd86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432140 #edb/ref 1074 "Dividing a whole number by a fraction is equivalent to multiplying the whole number by the reciprocal of the fraction.\n\nFirst, we write $8$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n8=\\frac{8}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we note that the reciprocal of $\\frac{3}{7}$ is $\\frac{7}{3}.$ \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n8÷\\frac{3}{7}=\\frac{8}{1}÷\\frac{3}{7}=\\frac{8}{1}\\times \\frac{7}{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply our fractions. \n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{1}\\times \\frac{7}{3}=\\frac{8\\times 7}{1\\times 3}=\\frac{56}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{56}{3}=18\\,\\text{R}\\,2=18\\,\\frac{2}{3}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432141 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432141 #edb/ref 1069 "q-211429" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432141 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432141 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed as an improper fraction in its lo